WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:01.620
KATARINA STANLEY: Welcome

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:04.590
to this session on
Anyscale on Azure.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.650
I'm Katarina, a Product
Marketing Manager at Anyscale,

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:10.170
and I'm joined today by my
colleague Daniel Arrizza,

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.190
a Field Engineer here.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:14.190
Quick agenda.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.250
I'll cover why teams are
choosing to own their AI stack,

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.770
what makes that hard to
scale, and how Anyscale

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:21.690
in Azure addresses it.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:23.760
Daniel's going to follow
with a technical demo,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.210
and we'll close with
some next steps.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.460
To jump right in,
every team building

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.120
with AI today is making
a choice on a spectrum.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.430
On one end, you can
rent intelligence,

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.820
calling someone else's
model through an API.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.710
On the other end,
you can own it.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.750
And owning is not binary.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.810
It's a spectrum that includes
deploying open-source models,

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.540
customizing open-source
models with your own data,

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.240
and training your own models.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.270
Most teams use a mix of
both renting and owning.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.340
The question is when
ownership makes sense

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.010
for a given workload.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:00.870
So, when is that the case?

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.540
It usually comes down
to a few things.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.840
Control over your models
and your operations,

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.210
so you're not exposed
to model deprecations,

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.970
rate limits or outages.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.130
Data and differentiation,
when you need

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:17.760
to keep proprietary data
inside your boundary.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.360
And cost and latency, for
better unit economics at scale

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.540
and faster end-to-end
response times.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.750
When one or more of these
apply, that's typically

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:31.980
when we see teams move
towards ownership.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.560
But here is the challenge point.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.350
Building AI is getting
harder because both data

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.990
and models are scaling fast.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.500
What used to be structured
tabular data is now multimodal.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.860
Data set sizes have moved
from gigabytes to petabytes.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.660
Single models are
now many models,

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.710
and parameter counts are
growing exponentially.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.240
Across the AI lifecycle,

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.000
that complexity is
showing up everywhere.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.660
Building requires coordinated
CPU and GPU compute.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.940
Training must be
distributed across nodes,

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:05.280
with fault tolerance built in.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.950
And serving requires
composing many models,

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.140
often on different hardware.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.440
These are problems legacy
tools were not designed for.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.400
And teams are increasingly
hitting their limits.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.280
It's why we're seeing
teams converge on Ray,

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.500
the open-source distributed
compute framework

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.080
for Python and AI workloads.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:26.820
As AI workloads
have grown in scale

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.790
and complexity, so
has Ray adoption.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.310
It has over 12 million
downloads per week

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:35.250
and it's the most widely
adopted AI compute framework

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:38.940
across both AI labs and
enterprises used by teams

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:43.310
like OpenAI, Uber,
TripAdvisor and more.

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:46.820
So, this brings us to Anyscale
on Azure from the creators

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:50.300
of Ray and powered
by Ray on AKS.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:52.020
Three core things to know.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.980
First, it accelerates dev to
prod with a unified runtime

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.130
for multimodal data curation,
distributed training

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:02.100
and multimodal online surveying.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.890
Second, it runs
reliably at scale

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:08.250
with a fully managed
Ray lifecycle on AKS,

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:11.910
a priority aware scheduler
that maximizes GPU utilization

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:15.090
and 24/7 Ray expert support.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.010
And third, it's Azure native.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.530
It runs on AKS in your
own subscription,

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.560
integrates with Entra ID
for RBAC and is available

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.150
through the Azure portal.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:27.180
So, your data and IP stay
inside your existing security

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.520
and governance boundaries.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.040
So, with that, I'm going to hand
